I've always been a pretty streaky worker. I go through manic phases where I have trouble doing anything (including sleep) other than working on a problem till I finish it. During these times I immediately wake up thinking about the problem, and rush back to my computer. This can last weeks, and I never tire. Burnout only happens to me after the political fallout from the outcome of my work. Once I get something "complete" though I settle back to slack off and enjoy the thing. I've always worked this way.

In my career I've had times when I built for differing sizes of an audience, and the manic streak could continue post-release as I took in feedback and wanted to please users. This was certainly the case for Giant Bomb, where I'd scour the message boards any time we released a feature. Elastic ran on a saner "release train", which meant I couldn't get fixes in till the next window, which acted as a forced regulator to my mania. Its only downside was that it usually led to procrastination, which was tough for library teams (like mine) that needed real oversight of others.

In general though I've always enjoyed these manic times in my life, and feel nostalgic for those periods in my career. In my youth there was likely some bipolar depression that came as a result of it, but once I settled into raising a family and had stronger relationships outside of work, that depressive hangover largely evaporated. It could be that you just gain perspective as you age. I don't have such high expectations for the fruit of my labor anymore, and have learned to appreciate the labor itself as the joy. Likely it's just easier as you become more financially stable. It's easy to be manic and take risks when you don't need to worry about the bills.

What I do worry about is how coworkers had to navigate around me during these periods. Often my work would require help, and manics love to force their priorities on others. Sometimes I'd find fellow manics, and we'd buzz the hive like worker bees passing code back and forth as if they were high-fives. Others, I'm sure, would tire just watching the madness from afar, hoping they didn't get stung. Having witnessed the symptoms in others, it can be like watching an evangelical speaking in tongues; their mania can physically change a person's speech. They talk faster and assume you share in their piety. This is why mania is tolerated best in cults.

My favorite Rands in Repose essay talked similarly about [Stables and Volatiles](https://randsinrepose.com/archives/stables-and-volatiles/) and what happens after 1.0 ships. It's easy to assume that streaky manics like me are always volatile workers, and that's true most of the time, but not always. Maintenance sometimes requires the occasional burst of energy to break out of technical debt, and I've shifted into these roles occasionally, even for years at a time.

What was harder for me was how to be a manager knowing my own work habits. I didn't ever want to suggest destructive behaviors, so I kept my own mania secret during these periods. Just because I like working this way, doesn't mean it was culturally acceptable when working in groups. The tension I felt was akin to... _Don't overwork on that thing you love, and expect me to rise to that level_. Yes, we should all think of our job as a transactional contract between the company and a worker, but by all means, keep building till midnight if that's what gives you happiness. Don't limit yourself because of others. Often, you'll get a nice side effect later of getting better pay because of it. Just don't force your work habits on others.

Being a manic in groups was what always led me to eventually leave and start something new on my own or in a protected group of like-minded crazies. Once a thing became successful and solved, I could feel the moon shift, until the werewolf would need to appear again. Thus, the cycle would continue.

I guess the only way to judge the usefulness of your mania is the legacy of what you build and the relationships with past coworkers. Does the thing you built still have a use? I have several open source libraries I've created that are still popular, and continue just fine without me. The same can be said for whole companies I built, or helped grow and then walked away from. I even had one instance where I built something, only to return a few years later to help them build it again after they floundered. Like most technology though, any project becomes tissue paper eventually. Either they were bad ideas, or were too hard to maintain, or both. Sometimes the world just sneezes and decides they don't need that thing anymore.

What I do believe is that some manner of obsession always needs to exist in the beginning of a project. A divine genesis or spark. There's no other way to make it past the hump where a project looks too big to tackle on your own. The irrational side of your brain needs to win.

I'm writing about this topic because I started building something new, and the mania has returned. I'm writing this post fast just so that I can get back to it! Each time my mania swings back I feel rejuvenated and remember past states like this so felt it important to write some thoughts down. Steinbeck labelled this feeling in [Chapter 13 of "East of Eden"](https://www.26reads.com/list/64438-east-of-eden/east-of-eden/2/2) as "The Glory" and defines it as a man's purpose.

> Sometimes a kind of glory lights up the mind of a man. It happens to nearly everyone. You can feel it growing or preparing like a fuse burning toward dynamite. It is a feeling in the stomach, a delight of the nerves, of the forearms. The skin tastes the air, and every deep-drawn breath is sweet. Its beginning has the pleasure of a great stretching yawn; it flashes in the brain and the whole world glows outside your eyes. A man may have lived all of his life in the gray, and the land and trees of him dark and somber. The events, even the important ones, may have trooped by faceless and pale. And then — the glory — so that a cricket song sweetens his ears, the smell of the earth rises chanting to his nose, and dappling light under a tree blesses his eyes. Then a man pours outward, a torrent of him, and yet he is not diminished. And I guess a man’s importance in the world can be measured by the quality and number of his glories. It is a lonely thing but it relates us to the world. It is the mother of all creativeness, and it sets each man separate from all other men.

Like Steinbeck, I believe we all have a glory. Sometimes that creativity can be as mundane as building a new website or app. It matters not what you build, but that you listen to the pull to build it, and let that glory envelop you in its warmth. At least — well — that's what I tell my manic and I.
